# paq8-C0.idx -- ContextModel's order hashes.
#
# cxt[1..13] are the order-1..order-13 context hashes, each one the previous
# order's hash times a multiplier plus the new byte.  Those thirteen
# multipliers are the whole order model: they decide which two different
# histories are allowed to land in the same ContextMap row, and they were a
# bare array of primes with nothing saying why those primes.
#
# Nine bits each, which is one more than eight of them need.  A multiplier here
# is not a mask -- its top bit is not a window edge -- so there is no reason to
# pin the width to what the shipped value happens to spell, and 257 already
# needs the ninth bit.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE
#
# The two mixer context selectors that ContextModel::p still spells out --
# MIX_R3's min(bpos,5)*256+(tt&63)+(c&192) and MIX_R5's bpos*256+... -- keep
# their constants.  Those numbers are not choices in the sense the rest of this
# tree uses: they are the RANGE of a selector, and the six ranges must sum
# exactly to MIXER_ROWS, which is a fixed array dimension of Mixer::wx.  Widen
# the tt mask by one bit and the sum overruns wx; the tuning build would catch
# it in Mixer::set and the shipping build would not, because MIXER_ROWS is a
# literal there.  That makes them the same category as P_BITS in paq8-G0.idx --
# the arithmetic rather than the model -- and handing them to opt.pl would be
# handing it the shape of the mixer.

Prefix C0
Debug 1
Const 0

Number PRIME1,  1, 0!100000001
Number PRIME2,  1, 0!011111011
Number PRIME3,  1, 0!011110001
Number PRIME4,  1, 0!011101111
Number PRIME5,  1, 0!011101001
Number PRIME6,  1, 0!011100101
Number PRIME7,  1, 0!011100011
Number PRIME8,  1, 0!011011111
Number PRIME9,  1, 0!011010011
Number PRIME10, 1, 0!011000111
Number PRIME11, 1, 0!011000101
Number PRIME12, 1, 0!011000001
Number PRIME13, 1, 0!010111111
